---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Winter pool care in Los Angeles: what your pool needs | GK Swimming Pools"
  description="Fall and winter pool care for Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley: leaves, rain, pump run times, cold nights, and heater checks. What your pool needs now."
  canonical="https://gkswimmingpools.com/blog/winter-pool-care/"
>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Winter Pool Care in Los Angeles: What Your Pool Needs When It Cools Off",
  "description": "Fall and winter pool care for Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley: leaves, rain, pump run times, cold nights, and heater checks. What your pool needs now.",
  "datePublished": "2026-11-07",
  "author": {
    "@type": "LocalBusiness",
    "name": "GK Swimming Pools",
    "telephone": "(818) 804-8204"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "LocalBusiness",
    "name": "GK Swimming Pools",
    "url": "https://gkswimmingpools.com"
  },
  "image": "https://gkswimmingpools.com/images/blog/winter-pool-care-hero.webp",
  "mainEntityOfPage": {
    "@type": "WebPage",
    "@id": "https://gkswimmingpools.com/blog/winter-pool-care/"
  }
})} />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need to close my pool for winter in Los Angeles?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Pools in Los Angeles, Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley stay open all year. Our winters are mild, so there's no need to drain or cover the pool the way people do back east. The pool still needs regular care, though. Leaves, rain and cold nights all affect the water, and a pool that sits without service through winter can turn green before spring."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long should I run my pool pump in winter?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Algae grows slower in cool water, so many pools can run fewer hours in winter than in summer. The water should still turn over at least once a day so it gets filtered and the chlorine gets mixed in. On nights with a freeze warning, the pump should run to keep water moving through the pipes. Many automation systems do this on their own with freeze protection."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I still need weekly pool service in the winter?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Winter brings fewer swimmers but more leaves, more rain and colder water, and all three throw off the chemistry. Weekly service keeps the baskets clear, the water balanced and the equipment checked, so a small problem doesn't turn into a green pool or a damaged pump by spring. Our weekly pool service starts at $150/mo with chemicals included."
      }
    }
  ]
})} />

  <section class="blog-hero">
    <div class="blog-hero__bg">
      <img src="/images/blog/winter-pool-care-hero.webp"
        alt="Clear, balanced pool water in the fall after weekly pool service by GK Swimming Pools"
        fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1200" height="900" />
    </div>
    <div class="blog-hero__overlay"></div>
    <div class="container blog-hero__inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">Pool Maintenance Guide</span>
      <h1>Winter Pool Care in Los Angeles: What Your Pool Needs When It Cools Off</h1>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span>GK Swimming Pools</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>November 7, 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="blog-body">

        <div class="blog-intro">
          <p>Nobody swims much in November, but your pool doesn't take the winter off. In Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley, fall and winter bring leaves, rain and cold nights, and each one affects your water and your equipment. Here's what your pool needs when the weather cools off.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Year Round</span>
          <h2>Pools in Los Angeles Stay Open All Year</h2>
          <p>Our winters are mild, so there's no need to drain or cover your pool the way people do in colder states. That also means the pool keeps running, and the water keeps needing attention.</p>
          <p>A pool that goes a few weeks without care in winter can look fine, then turn cloudy or green the first warm week of spring. Keeping it balanced through the cool months is easier and cheaper than cleaning up after it.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Leaves and Debris</span>
          <h2>Fall Leaves Do More Than Clog the Skimmer</h2>
          <p>Leaves pile up in the skimmer and pump baskets, which cuts flow and makes the pump work harder. As they break down in the water, they also add phosphates, and phosphates feed algae. In our experience, Santa Clarita pools near the canyons and hillsides get hit hardest this time of year.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>Empty the baskets often.</strong> Full skimmer and pump baskets restrict flow and strain the pump.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Skim before leaves sink.</strong> Leaves on the bottom stain plaster and break down faster.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Watch the filter.</strong> Leaf season loads up the filter. If the pressure climbs, read our guide on <a href="/blog/pool-filter-pressure-high/">pool filter pressure that's too high</a>.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Pump and Cold Nights</span>
          <h2>Pump Run Times and Freeze Protection</h2>
          <p>Algae grows slower in cool water, so many pools can run fewer hours in winter than in summer. The water should still turn over at least once a day so it gets filtered and the chlorine gets mixed in.</p>
          <p>Cold nights are the bigger risk. Santa Clarita and parts of the valley see near freezing temperatures a few nights each winter. Water that sits still in the pipes and equipment can freeze and crack them. Moving water doesn't freeze as easily.</p>
          <div class="callout-warn">
            <p>On nights with a freeze warning, make sure your pump runs overnight. Many automation systems have freeze protection that turns the pump on automatically when it gets cold. If you're not sure whether yours does, ask us to check it before the first cold snap.</p>
          </div>
          <p>If your pump is loud, slow to prime or tripping the breaker, winter is a good time to deal with it. See our guide to <a href="/blog/swimming-pool-pump-repair/">swimming pool pump repair</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Rain</span>
          <h2>Test the Water After Every Storm</h2>
          <p>Rain dilutes your chlorine, can shift your pH, and washes dirt, leaves and fertilizer into the pool. A big storm can undo a week of balancing overnight. Test the water and empty the baskets after heavy rain, and shock the pool if the chlorine drops. If the water turns hazy, our guide on <a href="/blog/cloudy-pool-water/">what causes cloudy pool water</a> walks through the fixes.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Heaters</span>
          <h2>Fire Up the Heater Before You Need It</h2>
          <p>If you plan to heat the pool or spa for the holidays, turn the heater on now, not the day guests arrive. A heater that sat all summer can have problems that only show up when it's asked to fire. Finding out early gives you time to get it repaired.</p>
          <p>If it won't turn on, or it runs but doesn't heat, start with our guide on <a href="/blog/pool-heater-not-working/">what to do when your pool heater isn't working</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Next Steps</span>
          <h2>Weekly Pool Service Through the Winter</h2>
          <p>Our <a href="/services/pool-maintenance/">weekly pool service</a> keeps your pool ready all year. Every visit covers skimming, brushing, vacuuming, emptying the baskets, testing and balancing the water, and a check of your pump, filter and heater. You get a written report after every visit. Weekly service starts at $150/mo with chemicals included.</p>
          <p>We're a family run company, three generations in, serving the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita.</p>

          <div class="blog-cta-inline">
            <a href="/services/pool-maintenance" class="btn btn--primary">Pool Maintenance Service</a>
            <a href="tel:8188048204" class="btn btn--outline">(818) 804-8204</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Keep Your Pool Clear This Winter</h2>
      <p>Weekly pool service from $150/mo, chemicals included. Serving the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita.</p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get In Touch</a>
    </div>
  </div>

</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-hero { position:relative; height:460px; overflow:hidden; display:flex; align-items:flex-end; }
  .blog-hero__bg { position:absolute; inset:0; }
  .blog-hero__bg img { width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 35%; display:block; }
  .blog-hero__overlay { position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top, rgba(6,47,97,.90) 0%, rgba(6,47,97,.35) 60%, rgba(6,47,97,.12) 100%); }
  .blog-hero__inner { position:relative; z-index:2; padding-bottom: var(--space-12); }
  .blog-hero__inner .eyebrow { color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-hero__inner h1 { color:var(--color-white); font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3.6vw,2.6rem); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); max-width:820px; }
  .blog-meta { display:flex; align-items:center; gap: var(--space-3); color:rgba(255,255,255,.72); font-size:var(--text-sm); font-weight:600; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .blog-meta__dot { width:4px; height:4px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-aqua); }

  .blog-body { max-width:760px; margin:0 auto; }
  .blog-intro { background:var(--color-mist); border-left:4px solid var(--color-aqua); padding:var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin-bottom:var(--space-12); border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0; }
  .blog-intro p { font-size:var(--text-lg); color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; line-height:1.7; margin:0; }
  .blog-section { margin-bottom:var(--space-12); }
  .blog-section .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-section h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .blog-section p { color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.85; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .blog-section p a, .blog-check-list a { color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; text-decoration:underline; text-underline-offset:3px; }
  .blog-section strong { color:var(--color-deep); }
  .blog-check-list { list-style:none; margin: var(--space-4) 0; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-check-list li { display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap: var(--space-3); font-size:var(--text-base); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; }
  .blog-check-list li::before { content:"\2713"; color:var(--color-aqua); font-weight:800; font-size:1rem; flex-shrink:0; margin-top:2px; }
  .blog-divider { border:none; border-top:2px solid var(--color-border); margin:var(--space-12) 0; }

  /* REPAIR STEPS */
  .step-list { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:var(--space-3); margin:var(--space-6) 0; }
  .step-item {
    display:flex; gap:var(--space-4); align-items:flex-start;
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding:var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
  }
  .step-item__num {
    flex-shrink:0; width:38px; height:38px; border-radius:50%;
    background:var(--color-aqua); color:var(--color-deep);
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:800; font-size:1.05rem;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; line-height:1;
  }
  .step-item__body h3 {
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-base);
    color:var(--color-deep); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.03em;
    margin-bottom:var(--space-2);
  }
  .step-item__body p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; margin:0; }

  /* WARNING CALLOUT */
  .callout-warn {
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left:3px solid #e8a33d; border-radius:var(--radius-sm);
    padding:var(--space-6); margin:var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .callout-warn p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.8; margin:0; }

  .blog-tip { background:var(--color-deep); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin: var(--space-8) 0; }
  .blog-tip__label { font-size:var(--text-xs); font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.15em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); font-family:var(--font-heading); }
  .blog-tip p { color:rgba(255,255,255,.88); margin:0; }

  .blog-cta-inline { display:flex; gap: var(--space-4); flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top: var(--space-8); }
  .btn--outline { display:inline-block; border:2px solid var(--color-deep); color:var(--color-deep); font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-sm); letter-spacing:.06em; text-transform:uppercase; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-8); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); text-decoration:none; }

  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .blog-hero { height:360px; }
    .blog-intro { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .step-item { gap:var(--space-4); padding:var(--space-4); }
    .blog-cta-inline { flex-direction:column; }
  }
</style>
